WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.772
Dennis keeps his job at a 60-employee firm after turning 65.

00:00:03.772 --> 00:00:08.230
Under the Medicare secondary payer rules, when an active employee or spouse age

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:12.688
65 or older is covered by a group plan of an employer with

00:00:12.688 --> 00:00:17.146
20 or more employees, the group plan pays first and Medicare pays second.

00:00:17.146 --> 00:00:19.889
If the employer has fewer than 20 employees,

00:00:19.889 --> 00:00:22.632
Medicare pays first and the group plan second.

00:00:22.632 --> 00:00:25.033
Retiree coverage is always secondary to Medicare.

00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:29.148
Because his employer is large, Dennis may delay Part B without penalty

00:00:29.148 --> 00:00:32.920
and enroll during the eight-month special enrollment period after he retires.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:34.041
Two more rules.

00:00:34.041 --> 00:00:38.525
A person under 65 who has Medicare because of disability and is

00:00:38.525 --> 00:00:43.009
covered by a group plan of an employer with 100 or more

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:47.494
employees, through her own or a family member’s current employment, has the

00:00:47.494 --> 00:00:51.604
group plan primary and Medicare secondary; under 100, Medicare pays first.

00:00:51.604 --> 00:00:56.836
For end-stage renal disease, the group plan is primary for a 30-month coordination period

00:00:56.836 --> 00:01:01.694
beginning with Medicare eligibility, regardless of employer size, after which Medicare becomes primary.

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:05.057
Employers may not offer incentives to drop group coverage

00:01:05.057 --> 00:01:08.420
in favor of Medicare or treat Medicare-eligible employees differently.

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.639
Medicaid is the joint federal and state program that pays

00:01:12.639 --> 00:01:16.859
for health care for people with limited income and resources.

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:21.078
In Maryland it is called Medical Assistance and administered by

00:01:21.078 --> 00:01:25.298
the Department of Health, with federal rules and matching funds.

00:01:25.298 --> 00:01:31.205
Since the Affordable Care Act expansion, adults with household income up to 138 percent

00:01:31.205 --> 00:01:37.112
of the poverty level qualify on income alone; children, pregnant women and parents have

00:01:37.112 --> 00:01:42.597
higher thresholds, and the Maryland Children’s Health Program covers children in moderate-income families.

00:01:42.597 --> 00:01:47.239
Eligibility for long-term care coverage adds a resource test with a

00:01:47.239 --> 00:01:51.880
low asset limit and a five-year look-back on transfers of assets.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:57.597
Medicaid covers mandatory benefits in every state: inpatient and outpatient hospital care,

00:01:57.597 --> 00:02:03.313
physician services, laboratory and x-ray, nursing facility care, home health, family planning,

00:02:03.313 --> 00:02:08.077
transportation to care and comprehensive screening and treatment for children.

00:02:08.077 --> 00:02:13.794
Maryland adds optional benefits including prescription drugs, dental for adults and home

00:02:13.794 --> 00:02:19.034
and community-based services that help people stay out of nursing homes.

00:02:19.034 --> 00:02:24.750
Medicaid is the payer of last resort: every other coverage pays first.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:30.943
People eligible for both Medicare and Medicaid are dual eligibles, and Medicaid or

00:02:30.943 --> 00:02:36.660
a Medicare Savings Program may pay their Medicare premiums and cost sharing.

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:39.394
Gloria wants to know who will pay if

00:02:39.394 --> 00:02:42.127
she needs to live in a nursing home.

00:02:42.127 --> 00:02:44.861
First, ask what kind of care she needs.

00:02:44.861 --> 00:02:48.961
In Original Medicare, Part A can cover up to 100 days of

00:02:48.961 --> 00:02:53.062
skilled nursing care in a benefit period if she meets the requirements.

00:02:53.062 --> 00:02:57.845
That usually includes a hospital stay of at least three days as an inpatient.

00:02:57.845 --> 00:03:00.237
She shares the cost after day 20.

00:03:00.237 --> 00:03:03.312
Help with bathing, dressing or eating alone is different.

00:03:03.312 --> 00:03:06.046
Medicare generally does not cover that ongoing help.

00:03:06.046 --> 00:03:10.146
Maryland Medicaid can help if she meets its financial and medical requirements.

00:03:10.146 --> 00:03:12.880
A private long-term care policy may also pay.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:19.230
Dennis, 66 and working for a large employer, has his group plan primary and

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:25.580
Medicare secondary; when he retires, Medicare becomes primary and a supplement fills the gaps.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:29.662
His brother, disabled at 58 and covered through a

00:03:29.662 --> 00:03:33.290
spouse’s 500-employee company, has the group plan primary.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:39.186
Gloria, 71 and on Medicare, can use Part A for a qualifying hospital

00:03:39.186 --> 00:03:45.083
stay and up to 100 days of skilled nursing care per benefit period.

00:03:45.083 --> 00:03:49.618
For a year of help with daily activities, she might

00:03:49.618 --> 00:03:54.154
use long-term care insurance, pay privately, or qualify for Medicaid.

00:03:54.154 --> 00:04:00.050
Lesson 47 builds that policy, and Lesson 48 adds Maryland’s rules for it.
